## Preliminaries: scope each workflow

Each example below needs its own session. This small helper starts one and
closes it in `after`, so a failed assertion also releases it. The callback's
result is returned as an ordinary Elixir value.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
project = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)

with_session = fn backend, fun ->
  {:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)

  try do
    fun.(session)
  after
    SWI.stop(session)
  end
end
```

## Measure startup and the first query

A Port session starts an OS process each time. The first NIF session
initializes VM-wide SWI; later sessions reuse that runtime. The measurements
include the first successful query, in microseconds.

```elixir
startup =
  for backend <- [:port, :nif], attempt <- 1..3 do
    {elapsed, {:ok, true}} =
      :timer.tc(fn ->
        with_session.(backend, &SWI.call(&1, "true"))
      end)

    %{backend: backend, attempt: attempt, lifecycle_us: elapsed}
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(startup, keys: [:backend, :attempt, :lifecycle_us])
```

The helper also closes the session before returning, so `lifecycle_us` includes
cleanup. The order is fixed and caches, scheduling, and prior NIF use matter.
Rerunning the cell does not measure a fresh VM's first NIF initialization.

## Describe the workloads

The tiny goal measures a small round trip. The 100-answer relation also
exercises streaming and decoding. The two proof tasks adapt
[Otten's tutorial](https://jens-otten.de/tutorial_tableaux19/examples/): a
four-step function chain and four pigeons occupying three holes. Formula
construction is visible here; solving uses the real vendored provers.

```elixir
chain_formula = "(p(a),(all X:(p(X)=>p(f(X)))))=>p(f(f(f(f(a)))))"

placements =
  for pigeon <- 1..4,
      do: "(" <> Enum.map_join(1..3, ";", &"o#{pigeon}#{&1}") <> ")"

collisions =
  for hole <- 1..3,
      first <- 1..4,
      second <- 1..4,
      first < second,
      do: "(o#{first}#{hole},o#{second}#{hole})"

pigeon_formula =
  "(" <> Enum.join(placements, ",") <> ")=>(" <> Enum.join(collisions, ";") <> ")"

quote_formula = fn formula -> formula |> :json.encode() |> IO.iodata_to_binary() end

workloads = [
  {"Tiny goal", "true", 1},
  {"100 solutions", "between(1,100,X)", 100},
  {"leanHaT function chain", "once(leanhat:prove_text(#{quote_formula.(chain_formula)}))",
   1},
  {"nanoCoP HT pigeonhole",
   "once(nanocop:prove_text(#{quote_formula.(pigeon_formula)},ht))", 1}
]

Kino.DataTable.new(
  for {name, goal, answers} <- workloads,
      do: %{workload: name, goal: goal, expected_answers: answers}
)
```

## Measure repeated work

Reuse one session per backend. Load only the shared integration adapters;
they contain no task-specific formulas. Check each workload once before
measurement and check the answer count during every timed run.

```elixir
timings =
  for backend <- [:port, :nif] do
    with_session.(backend, fn session ->
      for adapter <- ["leanhat.pl", "nanocop.pl"] do
        :ok = SWI.load(session, Path.join([project, "examples", adapter]))
      end

      for {name, goal, expected} <- workloads do
        assert Enum.count(SWI.query(session, goal)) == expected

        samples =
          for _ <- 1..7 do
            {elapsed, count} = :timer.tc(fn -> Enum.count(SWI.query(session, goal)) end)
            assert count == expected
            elapsed
          end

        sorted = Enum.sort(samples)

        %{
          backend: backend,
          workload: name,
          samples: length(sorted),
          median_us: Enum.at(sorted, div(length(sorted), 2)),
          p95_us: Enum.at(sorted, ceil(length(sorted) * 0.95) - 1)
        }
      end
    end)
  end
  |> List.flatten()

Kino.DataTable.new(timings, keys: [:workload, :backend, :median_us, :p95_us, :samples])
```

Times include query parsing, proof preprocessing, transport, and Elixir
decoding. These adapter calls do not install the kernel counters or return
proof terms used in [the proof-search chapter](02_provers.livemd).
Seven samples are a small illustration, not a reliable performance study.

### Try it yourself

Increase the sample count to 21. Predict which workload is most sensitive to
boundary costs, then compare median and p95 again.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The tiny goal does little Prolog work, so transport and scheduling can dominate.
The measured winner is machine-dependent. Both backends must still produce
the expected number of answers on every sample.

</details>

## Handle a search timeout

Timeouts are milliseconds per solution pull, excluding consumer time. A
cooperative SWI alarm timeout leaves the session usable with either backend.

```elixir
recovery =
  for backend <- [:port, :nif] do
    with_session.(backend, fn session ->
      {:error, :timeout} = SWI.call(session, "repeat,fail", timeout: 50)
      {:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "true")
      %{backend: backend, timed_out: true, usable_afterward: true}
    end)
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(recovery)
```

Port also has an external transport deadline with a one-second grace period.
If it fires, the child is killed and its database is lost. NIF cannot safely
kill arbitrary foreign code or cleanup handlers that do not service signals.
Use Port when a hard deadline or native crash isolation is required.

## Observe Port crash recovery

This example deliberately calls `halt/1` **only in a Port session**. It exits
the disposable SWI child. The next request starts a fresh child, so a fact
asserted before the crash is gone.

```elixir
crash_recovery =
  with_session.(:port, fn session ->
    {:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "assertz(notebook_marker(ready))")
    {:error, {:port_exit, 7}} = SWI.call(session, "halt(7)")
    {:ok, false} = SWI.call(session, "current_predicate(notebook_marker/1)")
    {:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "true")
    %{backend: :port, child_exit: 7, reconnected: true, previous_fact_retained: false}
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new([crash_recovery])
```

Calling `halt/1` through NIF would affect the entire BEAM VM. NIF sessions
also share ordinary modules and dynamic predicates, while Port sessions have
separate databases. These operational differences matter even though the
query API and term representation are shared.

## Session cleanup

Every example in this chapter uses the scoped helper, so there is no open
interactive session to close. Reevaluate a measurement cell directly to repeat
that workflow. The [getting-started chapter](01_basic.livemd) shows how to
retain one session across cells and close it explicitly.

